already be the\n\t\t\t// composed presentation object (and has been observed without a usable\n\t\t\t// third-party id). Looking it up by `active.id` then falls through to the\n\t\t\t// composed tokens and feeds our previous wallpaper wash back into the next\n\t\t\t// skin. Prefer the registered definition selected by `preference`; only use\n\t\t\t// active.id for `system`, where it resolves to the concrete light/dark theme.\n\t\t\tconst savedId = readSavedSkin();\n\t\t\tconst selectedId = typeof savedId === \"string\" && savedId !== DEFAULT_SKIN\n\t\t\t\t? savedId\n\t\t\t\t: snapshot.preference === \"system\"\n\t\t\t\t? snapshot.active?.id\n\t\t\t\t: snapshot.preference;\n\t\t\treturn snapshot.themes?.find((theme) => theme.id === selectedId)\n\t\t\t\t|| snapshot.themes?.find((theme) => theme.id === snapshot.active?.id)\n\t\t\t\t|| snapshot.active;\n\t\t}\n\n\t\tfunction applyCombinedTokenOverrides(ctx) {\n\t\t\tif (combinedOverrideApplying) return;\n\t\t\tcombinedOverrideApplying = true;\n\t\t\ttry {\n\t\t\t\tconst overrides = {\n\t\t\t\t\t...popupTokenOverrides,\n\t\t\t\t\t...accentTokenOverrides,\n\t\t\t\t\t...wallpaperTokenOverrides\n\t\t\t\t};\n\t\t\t\tif (Object.keys(overrides).length > 0) {\n\t\t\t\t\tconst previousDispose = combinedOverrideDispose;\n\t\t\t\t\tcombinedOverrideDispose = ctx.theme.overrideTokens(COMBINED_OVERRIDE_SOURCE, overrides);\n\t\t\t\t\tpreviousDispose?.();\n\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\tcombinedOverrideDispose?.();\n\t\t\t\t\tcombinedOverrideDispose = null;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t} finally {\n\t\t\t\tcombinedOverrideApplying = false;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\t/** The injected liquid-glass <style> node (leaf-card backdrop blur). */\n\t\tlet materialStyleEl = null;\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * WHICH copy of this module currently owns the page — deliberately kept at PAGE scope.\n\t\t *\n\t\t * Every page-scope handle below (`materialStyleEl`, the wallpaper node, the wash attribute\n\t\t * on `<html>`, the dialog alpha floor, the composer marker's observer + poll timers, the\n\t\t * wallpaper-refresh tick and its `visibilitychange` listener) is module-scope, and on a\n\t\t * long-lived page the bundle can be evaluated a SECOND time — measured live in the 10.5.0\n\t\t * audit: two `style#dsh-dream-skin-nav-icon` nodes in one DOM after hours of use. Two fibers\n\t\t * means two cleanups, and the host runs the old fiber's dispose effect whenever it likes. If\n\t\t * that order is \"new apply() first, old unload second\", an unguarded teardown walks the wall\n\t\t * and removes what the NEW generation just installed: the material sheet, the wallpaper, the\n\t\t * wash attribute, the readability floor and the composer marker — the page snaps to the\n\t\t * stock host look for as long as it takes the next repaint to re-apply. The marker was worse\n\t\t * than the rest: its `started` latch is module-scope too, so the stale stop did NOT get\n\t\t * re-applied by the next repaint (see `stopComposerMarker`), which is why the latch is now\n\t\t * cleared next to the observer.\n\t\t *\n\t\t * T9 solved the same problem for the nav-icon hook (`disposeNavHook(generation)`), and this\n\t\t * is the same lock, applied to everything else the fiber owns. `apply()` claims the page; a\n\t\t * cleanup that no longer holds the number does nothing.\n\t\t *\n\t\t * WHY THE NUMBER LIVES ON `window` AND NOT IN THIS MODULE. The premise of this whole block is\n\t\t * \"the bundle may be evaluated twice\" — and a second evaluation is a second module scope, so\n\t\t * a module-scope counter gives each copy its OWN numbering and the two can never see each\n\t\t * other: instance A's cleanup would compare against A's counter, find itself still the owner,\n\t\t * and retract `<html data-dsh-dream-skin-wash>` from under the live instance B. That is the\n\t\t * exact lesson this repository already wrote down for the nav hook in 10.5.x (CHANGELOG\n\t\t * \"导航钩子的所有权锁改为页面级 armed 位\": a per-instance boolean cannot arbitrate a shared\n\t\t * DOM node), and the wash attribute / sheet / floor ARE shared DOM nodes. So the claim is\n\t\t * stamped where every copy reads it. Same placement rule as the R-5 warn-dedupe set: when the\n\t\t * semantics are \"one per page\", the state goes on the page. If `window` is unreachable\n\t\t * (headless eval, a frozen global) the counter degrades to this instance only — the same\n\t\t * weaker guarantee the code had before, never a fabricated owner.\n\t\t */\n\t\tconst PAGE_OWNER_KEY = \"__DSH_DREAM_SKIN_PAGE_OWNER__\";\n\t\t/** Fallback owner number when `window` is not writable (per-instance, documented above). */\n\t\tlet modulePageOwner = 0;\n\t\tfunction readPageOwner() {\n\t\t\tlet value = NaN;\n\t\t\ttry { value = Number(window[PAGE_OWNER_KEY]); } catch { value = NaN; }\n\t\t\treturn Number.isFinite(value) ? value : modulePageOwner;\n\t\t}\n\t\t/** Claim the page for the calling `apply()` and hand back this generation's number. */\n\t\tfunction claimPageOwner() {\n\t\t\tconst next = readPageOwner() + 1;\n\t\t\tmodulePageOwner = next;\n\t\t\ttry { window[PAGE_OWNER_KEY] = next; } catch {}\n\t\t\treturn next;\n\t\t}\n\t\t/** True while nobody newer has taken the page. */\n\t\tfunction holdsPageOwner(owner) {\n\t\t\treturn readPageOwner() === owner;\n\t\t}\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * S-3 (external review 9.27.0 round 3): dispose handle for the ONE live\n\t\t * late-correction observer (R-2). The probe chain is per-`apply()`, so\n\t\t * without a module-scope handle a remount could leave a stale observer\n\t\t * watching `document.body` after fiber teardown — the opposite of this\n\t\t * project's \"no residue on unload\" tenet.\n\t\t *\n\t\t * T-2 (external review 9.27.0 round 4): this handle is FACTORY scope (one\n\t\t * per module instance), NOT page scope. \"At most one live observer\" therefore\n\t\t * holds per instance, and that is deliberate: R-5's warn-dedupe set went to\n\t\t * `window` because \"one cap per page\" IS its semantics, whereas two live\n\t\t * plugin instances on one page is already an abnormal double-load — not a\n\t\t * state worth widening the public `window` contract for.\n\t\t */\n\t\tlet lateCorrectionDispose = null;\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * T-1 (external review 9.27.0 round 4): dispose handle for the drift\n\t\t * ladder's OWN pending setTimeout. S-3 disarmed the late-correction\n\t\t * observer on unload, but the ladder was still invisible to teardown, so an\n\t\t * unmount inside the ~4.3s window let the remaining rounds keep rewriting\n\t\t * `checkedAt` — a consumer would then read a \"fresh\" snapshot from a plugin\n\t\t * that no longer exists. Same factory scope and same single-owner rule.\n\t\t */\n\t\tlet driftLadderDispose = null;\n\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * Parse a hex or rgb()/rgba() color into rgba() with the given alpha;\n\t\t * null when the input is not one of those two syntaxes.\n\t\t *\n\t\t * 10.5.1: split out of toRgba so the layer-2 driver can DETECT the\n\t\t * unparseable case instead of silently pinning the original value.\n\t\t * Pack themes may legally declare hsl()/hsla() (docs/themes-spec.md\n\t\t * accepts them via looksLikeColor), and the legacy fallback below\n\t\t * ships such a value through UNCHANGED — alpha included — so the\n\t\t * popup slider would read as \"dead\" on those skins.\n\t\t */\n\t\tfunction toRgbaStrict(color, alpha) {\n\t\t\tconst hex = /^#([0-9a-f]{3}|[0-9a-f]{6})$/i.exec(color.trim());\n\t\t\tif (hex !== null) {\n\t\t\t\tlet digits = hex[1];\n\t\t\t\tif (digits.length === 3) digits = digits.split(\"\").map((char) => char + char).join(\"\");\n\t\t\t\tconst n = parseInt(digits, 16);\n\t\t\t\treturn `rgba(${(n >> 16) & 255}, ${(n >> 8) & 255}, ${n & 255}, ${alpha})`;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst rgb = /^rgba?\\(\\s*(\\d+)\\s*,\\s*(\\d+)\\s*,\\s*(\\d+)(?:\\s*,\\s*([\\d.]+))?\\s*\\)$/i.exec(color.trim());\n\t\t\tif (rgb !== null) return `rgba(${rgb[1]}, ${rgb[2]}, ${rgb[3]}, ${alpha})`;\n\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t}\n\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * Legacy wrapper: parse, or return the input trimmed on failure (the\n\t\t *